{"task_id": "Mbpp/120", "solution": "def max_product_tuple(data: list[tuple[int | float, int | float]]) -> int | float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum absolute product from a list of two-number tuples.\n    Returns 0 if the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_abs_product = 0.0  # Initialize with a float to handle potential float results\n\n    for a, b in data:\n        current_product = a * b\n        current_abs_product = abs(current_product)\n        if current_abs_product > max_abs_product:\n            max_abs_product = current_abs_product\n            \n    return max_abs_product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "def search(array: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the element that appears only once in a sorted array.\n    This implementation uses a binary search approach, leveraging the sorted nature of the array.\n    \"\"\"\n    left, right = 0, len(array) - 1\n\n    while left <= right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n\n        # If mid is the unique element\n        # Check if mid is the first or last element, or if its neighbors are different\n        if (mid == 0 or array[mid - 1] != array[mid]) and \\\n           (mid == len(array) - 1 or array[mid] != array[mid + 1]):\n            return array[mid]\n\n        # Determine if the unique element is in the left or right half\n        # If mid is even, its pair should be at mid + 1\n        # If mid is odd, its pair should be at mid - 1\n        is_pair_on_right = (mid % 2 == 0 and array[mid] == array[mid + 1])\n        is_pair_on_left = (mid % 2 == 1 and array[mid] == array[mid - 1])\n\n        if is_pair_on_right or is_pair_on_left:\n            # The pair is correctly positioned, unique element is to the right\n            left = mid + 1\n        else:\n            # The pair is not correctly positioned, unique element is to the left\n            right = mid - 1\n\n    # This part should ideally not be reached if there is always a unique element\n    return -1 # Or raise an error, based on error handling policy. Given problem, should always find."}
