{"task_id": "Mbpp/256", "solution": "def count_Primes_nums(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of prime numbers strictly less than a given non-negative integer n.\n    Utilizes an internal helper function to check for primality.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if a number is prime.\n        Returns True if prime, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        if num < 2:\n            return False\n        for i in range(2, int(num**0.5) + 1):\n            if num % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    count = 0\n    for i in range(2, n):\n        if _is_prime(i):\n            count += 1\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/259", "solution": "def maximize_elements(tuple_collection_1: tuple, tuple_collection_2: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Maximizes elements from two tuple collections element-wise.\n    It iterates through corresponding inner tuples and their elements to find the maximum.\n    \"\"\"\n    maximized_collection = []\n    for inner_tuple_1, inner_tuple_2 in zip(tuple_collection_1, tuple_collection_2):\n        maximized_inner_tuple = []\n        for element_1, element_2 in zip(inner_tuple_1, inner_tuple_2):\n            maximized_inner_tuple.append(max(element_1, element_2))\n        maximized_collection.append(tuple(maximized_inner_tuple))\n    return tuple(maximized_collection)"}
