{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "def max_Product(arr: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a pair with the highest product from a given array of integers.\n    The function considers products of the two largest and two smallest (most negative) numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(arr) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input array must contain at least two elements to form a pair.\")\n\n    arr.sort()\n\n    # Candidates for highest product are:\n    # 1. Product of the two largest numbers\n    # 2. Product of the two smallest (most negative) numbers\n\n    # Get the two largest numbers\n    max1, max2 = arr[-1], arr[-2]\n    product_of_largest = max1 * max2\n    pair_of_largest = (max2, max1) # Return in sorted order as per example, or just (max1, max2)\n\n    # Get the two smallest numbers\n    min1, min2 = arr[0], arr[1]\n    product_of_smallest = min1 * min2\n    pair_of_smallest = (min1, min2)\n\n    if product_of_largest > product_of_smallest:\n        return pair_of_largest\n    else:\n        return pair_of_smallest"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/422", "solution": "def find_Average_Of_Cube(n):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the average of the cubes of the first 'n' natural numbers.\"\"\"\n    sum_of_cubes = 0\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        sum_of_cubes += i * i * i\n    return sum_of_cubes / n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/419", "solution": "import math\n\ndef _round_half_up(number: float) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Rounds a single floating-point number to the nearest integer, with halves rounding up.\n    This ensures .5 rounds away from zero (e.g., 1.5 -> 2, -1.5 -> -2).\n    \"\"\"\n    if number >= 0:\n        return int(math.floor(number + 0.5))\n    else:\n        return int(math.ceil(number - 0.5))\n\ndef round_and_sum(numbers: list[float]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Rounds every number in a list, sums the rounded values, and multiplies the sum by the list length.\n    The rounding rule for .5 is \"half up\" (away from zero).\n    \"\"\"\n    list_length = len(numbers)\n    sum_of_rounded_numbers = 0\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        sum_of_rounded_numbers += _round_half_up(num)\n\n    final_result = sum_of_rounded_numbers * list_length\n    return final_result"}
