{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(arr: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence within the input array.\n    Uses a dynamic programming approach to find the maximum product ending at each position.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0.0\n\n    n = len(arr)\n    # dp[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    dp = [float(x) for x in arr]\n    max_overall_product = max(dp) if dp else 0.0\n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * arr[i])\n        max_overall_product = max(max_overall_product, dp[i])\n\n    return max_overall_product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/471", "solution": "def find_remainder(array: list[int], n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the product of array elements modulo n.\n    This function iteratively applies the modulo operation to prevent large intermediate products.\n    \"\"\"\n    product = 1\n    for element in array:\n        product = (product * element) % n\n    return product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/470", "solution": "def add_pairwise(input_tuple: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the pairwise sum of neighboring elements in the given tuple.\n\n    Args:\n        input_tuple (tuple): A tuple of numerical elements (e.g., int, float).\n\n    Returns:\n        tuple: A new tuple containing the sums of adjacent elements.\n               The length of the output tuple is one less than the input tuple.\n               Returns an empty tuple if input_tuple has fewer than two elements.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If input_tuple is not a tuple.\n        TypeError: If elements within the tuple are not numerical and cannot be added.\n\n    Example:\n        >>> add_pairwise((1, 5, 7, 8, 10))\n        (6, 12, 15, 18)\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(input_tuple, tuple):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a tuple.\")\n\n    if len(input_tuple) < 2:\n        return ()\n\n    result_list = []\n    for i in range(len(input_tuple) - 1):\n        try:\n            # Attempt to add elements, relying on Python's dynamic typing\n            # to handle various numerical types (int, float).\n            # This implicitly checks if elements are numerical and support addition.\n            result_list.append(input_tuple[i] + input_tuple[i+1])\n        except TypeError as e:\n            raise TypeError(f\"Elements in the tuple must be numerical for addition: {e}\")\n\n    return tuple(result_list)"}
